input` message that lists what each script returned. Assertions that a message *contains* a phrase still hold, because the prefix keeps the text intact.\n\n## Line guard — at ai_review after rules a–j pass, and inside rule j\n\nThe line guard only ever sees a verdict that already parsed and passed rules a–i. A missing or unparseable verdict is caught earlier, by rule a, as `failed`. Every failure below yields `needs_input` with `## Verdict` removed, and a message of the form `<reason>; evidence:` followed by the verdict's `evidence` as a quote block (§ Quoting evidence).\n\n1. Resolve `<agent dir>/repos/<repo>/<path>` from the verdict's `repo` and `file:line`, following symlinks on every path component.\n2. Resolved path outside `<agent dir>/repos/<repo>/` (symlinked file or symlinked directory escaping the clone) → reason `%q resolves outside the clone` (the cited path).\n3. File absent → reason `%q not found in clone`.\n4. File has fewer than `<line>` lines → reason `%q beyond end of file (<n> lines)` (the cited `path:line`).\n5. Otherwise → pass. After ai_review: `done`, phase `done`, status `completed`. Inside rule j: continue to the `below High` escalation with the verdict kept.\n\nA line-guard failure means the cited line cannot be shown to exist, which the contract treats as \"no line can be cited\" — so the verdict is removed, as in rule c, and its evidence survives in `## Failure`.\n\n## Stale failure marker\n\nThe framework writes `## Failure` on `failed` / `needs_input` and never removes it. When the ai_review line guard passes (the task completes), the diagnoser removes any `## Failure` section from the delivered body, so a completed task never carries a failure from an earlier attempt. Git history of the task file keeps the earlier failure.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91ba6c49d09b87d8","type":"tool_result","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Benjamin Borbe All rights reserved.\n// Use of this source code is governed by a BSD-style\n// license that can be found in the LICENSE file.\n\n// Package prompts provides embedded prompt fragments for the Claude agent.\npackage prompts\n\nimport (\n\t_ \"embed\"\n\n\tclaudelib \"github.com/bborbe/agent/claude\"\n\t\"github.com/bborbe/vault-cli/pkg/domain\"\n)\n\n//go:embed planning.md\nvar planning string\n\n//go:embed execution.md\nvar execution string\n\n//go:embed ai_review.md\nvar aiReview string\n\n//go:embed output-format.md\nvar outputFormat string\n\n// BuildInstructions assembles the prompt for one diagnoser phase: that phase's\n// instructions followed by the shared output-format envelope.\nfunc BuildInstructions(phase domain.TaskPhase) claudelib.Instructions {\n\tvar phaseInstruction claudelib.Instruction\n\tswitch phase {\n\tcase domain.TaskPhasePlanning:\n\t\tphaseInstruction = claudelib.Instruction{Name: \"planning\", Content: planning}\n\tcase domain.TaskPhaseExecution:\n\t\tphaseInstruction = claudelib.Instruction{Name: \"execution\", Content: execution}\n\tcase domain.TaskPhaseAIReview:\n\t\tphaseInstruction = claudelib.Instruction{Name: \"ai_review\", Content: aiReview}\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn claudelib.Instructions{{Name: \"output-format\", Content: outputFormat}}\n\t}\n\treturn claudelib.Instructions{\n\t\tphaseInstruction,\n\t\t{Name: \"output-format\", Content: outputFormat},\n\t}\n}\n===PLANNING===\nYou are stage 1 of the defect lane, running its planning phase.\n\nRead the `## Observation` section of the task as the operator's report of a\nmisbehaving service. It contains no diagnosis, and you must not ask for one.\nYour job in this phase is to resolve that service to a repository and clone it.\n\n## Resolve the service\n\nResolve the service named in `## Observation` only by an exact, case-sensitive\nmatch against the `service_repo.<service>` entries listed in this prompt's\n`## Environment` section. The mapping is operator-supplied. The match is exact\nand case-sensitive: there is no fuzzy match, no fallback, and no repo taken\nfrom your own knowledge.\n\n## On a match\n\nRun:\n\n```\nscripts/repo-clone.sh clone <repo>\n```\n\nThen record in the `## Plan` section the four keys `service`, `repo`,\n`clone_path` and `head_sha`. Take `clone_path` and `head_sha` from the script's\nstdout.\n\n## On no match, or when the clone fails\n\nReturn the JSON envelope with `\"status\":\"needs_input\"`. Name the service you\ncould not resolve, or the clone error you saw. Never substitute a repo of your\nown choosing.\n\n## Response rules\n\nNever start a line of your response with #. The step supplies the `## Plan`\nheading.\n\nEnd your response with the JSON envelope defined by `<output-format>`, and put\nnothing after it.\n===EXEC===\nYou are stage 1 of the defect lane, running its execution phase.\n\n## Observe the running system\n\nObserve the running system in this order. First the task files, through\n`scripts/vault-read.sh` and `scripts/vault-list.sh`. Then metrics, through\n`scripts/prometheus-query.sh`.\n\nThese four read-only scripts are your only eyes on the running system. They\ncannot write, and you must not reach a cluster address directly.\n\n## Localise the cause\n\nLocalise the cause in the clone that planning left, using `Read`, `Grep` and\n`Glob`.\n\nIf the clone is absent (for example after a pod restart), re-clone it first\nwith `scripts/repo-clone.sh clone <repo>`.\n\n## Write the verdict\n\nEmit a fenced ```yaml block inside the `## Verdict` section, with the eight\nkeys in the contract's order:\n\n```yaml\ndefect_id: <copied from the task's frontmatter defect_id>\nservice: <the running service observed>\nrepo: <owner>/<repo>            # resolved in planning\nfile:line: <path>:<line>        # must resolve in the repo at the cloned ref\nroot_cause: <one paragraph>\nrecommended_fix: <one paragraph>\nevidence: <the live observation — script call + its output>\nconfidence: High | Medium | Low\n```\n\nCopy `defect_id` from the task's frontmatter. Never invent one.\n\nPut the exact script call and its output into `evidence`. A verdict whose\nevidence does not name a live read is rejected by the binary.\n\nGive a cause that is supported only by reading source a `confidence` below\n`High`. Reserve `High` for a cause the live reads show.\n\n## When no line can be cited\n\nReturn the JSON envelope with `\"status\":\"needs_input\"`, write no `## Verdict`\nsection at all, and make the message list each script call you tried and what\nthat call returned.\n\n## Response rules\n\nNever start a line of your response with #. The step supplies the `## Verdict`\nheading.\n\nEnd your response with the JSON envelope defined by `<output-format>`, and put\nnothing after it.\n===AIREVIEW===\nYou are stage 1 of the defect lane, running its review phase.\n\n## Re-check the verdict\n\nRe-check the `## Verdict` the execution phase wrote against the task's\n`## Observation`. Fail the check rather than wave it through.\n\nCheck four things:\n\n1. The verdict's `service` matches the service named in `## Observation`.\n2. `root_cause` explains the observed symptom.\n3. `evidence` quotes a script call and its output.\n4. The content of the line cited by `file:line` supports `root_cause`.\n\nIf the clone is absent, re-clone it with `scripts/repo-clone.sh clone <repo>`.\nRecord in the `## Review` section any `head_sha` difference from the `## Plan`\nsection.\n\n## On a failed check\n\nReturn the JSON envelope with `\"status\":\"needs_input\"`, naming the check that\nfailed.\n\n## On a passing check\n\nWrite the `## Review` section and return `\"status\":\"done\"`.\n\n## Response rules\n\nNever start a line of your response with #. The step supplies the `## Review`\nheading.\n\nEnd your response with the JSON envelope defined by `<output